E=[('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 4, '"I came that they may have life, and have it abundantly."\n', '"The thief comes only to steal and kill and destroy; I came that they may have life, and have it abundantly."\n'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 16, '"Truly, truly, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you... He who eats my flesh and drinks my blood abides in me, and I in him."', '"So Jesus said to them, \'Truly, truly, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you; he who eats my flesh and drinks my blood has eternal life, and I will raise him up at the last day.\'"'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 18, '\\hfill\\small — John 6:53, 56', '\\hfill\\small — John 6:53-54'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 31, 'Everyone now claims their own "truth":', 'Many now claim their own "truth":'), ("1 · The opening picks up Chapter 9's Bridge; communion is God's gift (item 6, grace first)", 37, "the costly self-gift that proves love is real. But sacrifice isn't an end in itself; it's the means by which love creates something: **communion.** This chapter", 'the costly self-gift that shows love is real. But sacrifice is never the goal. The goal is **communion**: the fruit of all four pillars together, truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice, the communion with God and neighbor we were made for. This chapter'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 46, 'This is *perichoresis*—mutual indwelling.', 'This is what the Church calls *perichoresis* (per-ee-kor-EE-sis): mutual indwelling.'), ('2 · God needs nothing, and the Three are one God (todos 39 and 61)', 54, 'This communion is perfect happiness. God created not out of need but out of the overflow of love—love that must give, must create, must multiply. Love that gives out of overflow, not obligation.', 'This communion is perfect happiness, lacking nothing. So God did not create because He had to. The Church is plain: creation "is not the product of any necessity whatever," but "proceeds from God\'s free will; he wanted to make his creatures share in his being, wisdom and goodness" (CCC 295). Creation is an act of love, not of power: love that gives out of overflow, not obligation.'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 56, 'This is what we\'re invited into: "That they may all be one; even as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be in us" (John 17:20-21).', 'This is what we\'re invited into: "that they may all be one; even as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be in us, so that the world may believe that thou hast sent me" (John 17:21).'), ("1 · The opening picks up Chapter 9's Bridge; communion is God's gift (item 6, grace first)", 58, "Not virtue for its own sake. Not self-fulfillment. But the self-giving of sacrifice creates communion. This is the true end of love—the goal toward which everything else points. You don't become good for your own sake; you live a life that's good so your communion with God overflows from you to others.", 'Not virtue for its own sake. Not self-fulfillment. Communion: the bond with God and neighbor that truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice, held together, bring to life. This is the true end of love—the goal toward which everything else points. God does not make you good for your own sake; He fills you with His own life so that your communion with Him overflows from you to others.'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 63, '*Redemptoris Missio* (1990), §89', '*Redemptoris Missio* (1990), §7'), ('6 · Said once: repeats within the chapter and from Chapters 6 and 9 (item 5)', 66, 'This is what Aquinas meant by beatitude, what Augustine meant', 'This is what St. Thomas Aquinas meant by beatitude, what St. Augustine meant'), ('6 · Said once: repeats within the chapter and from Chapters 6 and 9 (item 5)', 66, '"God, infinitely perfect and blessed in Himself, in a plan of sheer goodness freely created man to make him share in His own blessed life" (CCC 1)', '"God, infinitely perfect and blessed in himself, in a plan of sheer goodness freely created man to make him share in his own blessed life" (CCC 1)'), ('6 · Said once: repeats within the chapter and from Chapters 6 and 9 (item 5)', 66, 'Jesus prayed "that they may all be one; even as thou, Father, art in me, and I in thee, that they also may be in us" (John 17:21)—He\'s inviting us into the very communion of the Trinity, into the highest good itself.', 'This is the communion of the Trinity that Jesus prayed we would be drawn into: the highest good itself.'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 112, 'as the Father is in Him and He in the Father (John 17:21).', 'as the Father is in Him and He in the Father (cf. John 17:21).'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 114, 'When God commands "Be holy, for I, the LORD your God, am holy" (Leviticus 19:2), He\'s saying:', 'When God commands, "Say to all the congregation of the people of Israel, You shall be holy; for I the LORD your God am holy" (Leviticus 19:2), He\'s saying:'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 152, "This is why the Church insists that the unitive and procreative aspects of sexuality cannot be separated. They're two aspects", 'This is why the Church teaches "the inseparable connection, established by God, which man on his own initiative may not break, between the unitive significance and the procreative significance which are both inherent to the marriage act" (CCC 2366): the marital act both unites husband and wife and stays open to new life. They\'re two aspects'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 154, 'The Culture of Death our society has embraced', 'What St. John Paul II called the culture of death, which our society has embraced'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 154, 'Marriage exists for the children—to give them a mother and father united in covenant love, to welcome them into a communion of persons that images the Trinity.', 'Marriage "is by its nature ordered toward the good of the spouses and the procreation and education of offspring" (CCC 1601): it gives children a mother and father united in covenant love, and welcomes them into a communion of persons that images the Trinity.'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 158, 'Not everyone is called to physical procreation, but all are called to spiritual fruitfulness', 'Not everyone is called to physical procreation, and some spouses long for children who never come. To them the Church says: "Their marriage can radiate a fruitfulness of charity, of hospitality, and of sacrifice" (CCC 1654). All are called to spiritual fruitfulness'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 158, '"You did not choose me, but I chose you and appointed you that you should go and bear fruit and that your fruit should abide." (John 15:16).', '"You did not choose me, but I chose you and appointed you that you should go and bear fruit and that your fruit should abide; so that whatever you ask the Father in my name, he may give it to you." (John 15:16).'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 167, '"Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb" (Revelation 19:9)', '"And the angel said to me, \'Write this: Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb.\' And he said to me, \'These are true words of God.\'" (Revelation 19:9)'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 171, 'Pause here and consider what St. Paul is really saying: "Because there is one bread, we who are many are one body, for we all partake of the one bread" (1 Corinthians 10:17). This is not two separate truths', 'Pause here and consider what St. Paul is really saying in that verse (1 Corinthians 10:17). It is not two separate truths'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 171, "We don't receive Communion and then go form the Church. In receiving Communion, we *become* the Church.", 'The Church says it in five words: "the Eucharist makes the Church." Baptism first made us members of the one Body; Communion "renews, strengthens, and deepens this incorporation into the Church, already achieved by Baptism" (CCC 1396).'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 173, "It is the direct expression of God's love, created and sustained by the Eucharist itself.", "It is the Trinity's own unity shared with us (cf. CCC 813), given and renewed in the Eucharist (cf. CCC 1396)."), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 173, 'Remove any facet, and the communion fractures. Hold them together, and you have the Church', 'Held together, they give us the Church'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 177, 'This is some of the understanding that leads the Church to teach that the Eucharist is "the source and summit of our faith."', 'This is why the Church calls the Eucharist "the source and summit of the Christian life" (CCC 1324).'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 181, 'The Eucharist is the source and summit of our faith because', 'The Eucharist is the source and summit because'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 181, '(John 6:51). It is the Lamb', '(cf. John 6:51). It is the Lamb'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 181, '(John 1:29; Revelation 5:6)', '(cf. John 1:29; Revelation 5:6)'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 181, 'under the appearance of bread (Matthew 1:23)', 'under the appearance of bread (cf. Matthew 1:23)'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 181, 'the first and the last (Revelation 22:13)', 'the first and the last (cf. Revelation 22:13)'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 183, 'everything in Scripture suddenly makes sense—from Genesis ("It is not good for the man to be alone") to Revelation ("Behold, God\'s dwelling is with the human race"). The entire story', 'everything in Scripture suddenly makes sense. It opens with God\'s own verdict on being alone: "Then the LORD God said, \'It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him a helper fit for him.\'" (Genesis 2:18). It closes with communion at last: "and I heard a loud voice from the throne saying, \'Behold, the dwelling of God is with men. He will dwell with them, and they shall be his people, and God himself will be with them;\'" (Revelation 21:3). The entire story'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 189, 'This is the most intimate union possible—God entering', 'This is the most intimate union possible in this life—God entering'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 199, 'Blessed are those called to the [marriage] supper of the Lamb"', 'Blessed are those called to the supper of the Lamb"'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 199, 'It is the word Scripture reserves for its holiest hinges—"Behold, I am the handmaid of the Lord" (Luke 1:38); "Behold, the Lamb of God" (John 1:29); "Behold, I make all things new" (Revelation 21:5).', 'It is the word Scripture sets at its holiest hinges—"Behold, I am the handmaid of the Lord" (cf. Luke 1:38); "Behold, the Lamb of God" (cf. John 1:29); "Behold, I make all things new" (cf. Revelation 21:5).'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 201, 'That small "Amen" is your vow—a quiet "I do," spoken again. However tired you are, however far you may have wandered since the last time, you step forward and say yes once more to the One who has never stopped saying yes to you.', 'That small "Amen" is your vow—a quiet "I do," spoken again. St. Augustine told the newly baptized what their Amen means: "Be then a member of the Body of Christ that your Amen may be true" (quoted in CCC 1396). However tired you are, you step forward and say yes once more to the One who has never stopped saying yes to you. And if you have wandered far, His mercy has already made the way home: "Anyone conscious of a grave sin must receive the sacrament of Reconciliation before coming to communion" (CCC 1385).'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 201, 'Binding becomes not just theological truth but bodily reality—the same reality that makes a man and woman one in marriage.', 'Binding becomes not just theological truth but bodily reality—the great mystery that the one flesh of husband and wife was always pointing to (cf. Ephesians 5:31–32).'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 205, 'One host can be divided infinitely—every piece is the whole Christ.', 'One host can be broken, and every piece is the whole Christ (CCC 1377).'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 219, 'Every Sunday, in every Catholic church on earth —', 'Every Sunday, in Catholic churches across the earth —'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 219, 'so that we love Him with all four together (Mark 12:30)', 'so that we love Him with all four together (cf. Mark 12:30)'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 221, '"Lord have mercy, Christ have mercy, Lord have mercy."', '"Lord, have mercy. Christ, have mercy. Lord, have mercy."'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 223, 'Every Sunday a Catholic hears more Scripture in one Mass than most people read in a month — by design.', 'Across its three-year cycle of Sunday readings, the Church walks her people through the heart of all four Gospels — by design.'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 225, 'is to be sanctified in the truth Jesus prayed for in John 17.', 'is to be sanctified in the truth Jesus prayed for: "Sanctify them in the truth; thy word is truth" (John 17:17).'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 227, 'the Body asks the Father for what each of His children is owed.', 'the Body pleads before the Father for all who are denied what they are due.'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 231, 'The priest, *in persona Christi*, speaks', 'The priest, acting *in persona Christi* (in the person of Christ), speaks'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 231, 'Heaven opens. Past, present, and future converge. The Lamb', 'Heaven opens. The Lamb'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 237, "The Centurion's words (Matthew 8:8) — truth, mercy, and the recognition of God's infinite power to heal us, spoken in our last breath before He enters under our roof.", "The centurion's words (cf. Matthew 8:8) — truth, mercy, and the recognition of God's infinite power to heal us, our last words before He enters under our roof."), ('3 · “Four pillars,” not threads (item 1)', 239, 'the four threads woven into the one Body', 'the four pillars bound together in the one Body'), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 241, 'The Latin *missa* — from which we get the very word *Mass* — literally means **sent**. We are sent into the world', 'The Church explains the name: the liturgy "concludes with the sending forth (*missio*) of the faithful, so that they may fulfill God\'s will in their daily lives" (CCC 1332). We are **sent** into the world'), ('6 · Said once: repeats within the chapter and from Chapters 6 and 9 (item 5)', 245, 'This is why the first Christians were celebrating this liturgy before a single line of the New Testament was written; it was *at* that liturgy, *for* that worshiping Body, that the New Testament was written at all. For the New Covenant was never first a book—it is a Person: *"This cup which is poured out for you is the new covenant in my blood"* (Luke 22:20). This is something most readers never realize, and it changes everything: the very name *New Testament* means *New Covenant* (from the Latin *testamentum*, "covenant"), so to open the New Testament is already to read the inspired witness to the New Covenant who is Jesus—and the Mass is where that covenant is renewed among us. The Bible was born at the altar; the altar was not born from the Bible—which is why, far from missing the Gospel, the Catholic at Mass', 'The first Christians were breaking the bread together (cf. Acts 2:42) before a single line of the New Testament was written, and its books were written for those worshiping communities and read aloud when they gathered. As Chapter 6 showed, the New Covenant was first given at a table, in His Blood (cf. Luke 22:20), and only later gave its name to the books that witness to it. The Church says it in one sentence: she "has always venerated the divine Scriptures just as she venerates the body of the Lord, since, especially in the sacred liturgy, she unceasingly receives and offers to the faithful the bread of life from the table both of God\'s word and of Christ\'s body" (Second Vatican Council, *Dei Verbum* 21). The New Testament was born at the altar; the altar was not born from the New Testament—which is why the Catholic at Mass'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 247, 'under every regime — even her persecutors, even her martyrdoms — for two thousand years.', 'under every regime, through persecution and martyrdom, for two thousand years.'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 247, '**To go to Mass is to step into the only place on earth where all four pillars', '**To go to Mass is to step into the place where all four pillars'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 254, 'the pillar and foundation of truth (1 Timothy 3:15)', 'the pillar and bulwark of the truth (1 Timothy 3:15)'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 256, 'like a branch cut from the vine (John 15:5-6)', 'like a branch cut from the vine (cf. John 15:5-6)'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 258, 'When one member suffers, all suffer; when one rejoices, all rejoice (1 Corinthians 12:26).', '"If one member suffers, all suffer together; if one member is honored, all rejoice together" (1 Corinthians 12:26).'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 264, 'the Church has called her, from her earliest centuries, **Mother of Mercy** and **Refuge of Sinners**.', 'the Church has long called her **Mother of Mercy** (in the *Hail, Holy Queen*) and **Refuge of Sinners** (in the Litany of Loreto).'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 264, 'a soul pierced by the same sword that pierced His side (cf. Luke 2:35).', 'her own soul pierced by a sword, as Simeon foretold, in the hour His side was pierced (cf. Luke 2:35; John 19:34).'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 264, '*"Do whatever he tells you"* (John 2:5) — her words', '*"His mother said to the servants, \'Do whatever he tells you.\'"* (John 2:5) — her words'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 268, 'the souls in purgatory, are being purified,', 'the souls in purgatory, are being purified and made ready for heaven (cf. CCC 1030),'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 274, 'the sacred author calls it "a holy and pious thought"—"therefore he made atonement for the dead, that they might be delivered from their sin" (2 Maccabees 12:45).', 'the sacred author writes: "But if he was looking to the splendid reward that is laid up for those who fall asleep in godliness, it was a holy and pious thought. Therefore he made atonement for the dead, that they might be delivered from their sin" (2 Maccabees 12:45).'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 274, '"great cloud of witnesses" (Hebrews 12:1)', '"great cloud of witnesses" (cf. Hebrews 12:1)'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 276, 'Christ said as much Himself, to a grieving sister at her brother\'s tomb: *"I am the resurrection and the life; he who believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and whoever lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?"* (John 11:25–26).', 'To a grieving sister, on the way to her brother\'s tomb: *"Jesus said to her, \'I am the resurrection and the life; he who believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and whoever lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?\'"* (John 11:25–26).'), ('2 · God needs nothing, and the Three are one God (todos 39 and 61)', 286, 'not because uniformity is imposed upon them, but because each Person eternally gives Himself to the others in love. The Son does only what He sees the Father doing (John 5:19) and came "not to do my own will, but the will of him who sent me" (John 6:38); the Spirit speaks not on His own authority but takes what is the Son\'s and declares it (John 16:13-15).', 'not because uniformity is imposed upon them, and not because three agree, but because "each of them is God whole and entire" (CCC 253), each eternally given to the others in love. The Son does only what He sees the Father doing (cf. John 5:19): "For I have come down from heaven, not to do my own will, but the will of him who sent me;" (John 6:38). The Spirit speaks not on His own authority but takes what is the Son\'s and declares it (cf. John 16:13-15).'), ('3 · “Four pillars,” not threads (item 1)', 290, "missing one or more of love's four threads—and so it always fractures.** Walk through them one at a time, and you will see that no single thread can hold", "missing one or more of love's four pillars—and so, sooner or later, it fractures.** Walk through them one at a time, and you will see that no single pillar can hold"), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 292, 'suppressed reality always resurfaces', 'suppressed reality sooner or later resurfaces'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 294, 'the moment they can leave, they do.', 'the moment they can leave, they often do.'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 298, 'Sacrifice is the love that proves the bond is real', 'Sacrifice is the love that shows the bond is real'), ('3 · “Four pillars,” not threads (item 1)', 300, 'because the four threads are facets of one undivided Love. Remove any one and the others unravel with it—truth without mercy expels, mercy without truth enables, justice without sacrifice finds no one willing to pay,', 'because the four pillars are facets of one undivided Love. Remove any one and the others give way with it—truth without mercy expels, mercy without truth enables, justice without sacrifice finds no one willing to bear its cost,'), ('3 · “Four pillars,” not threads (item 1)', 300, 'in Him truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are not four ingredients held in careful balance but one seamless reality. The world keeps trying to build communion while cutting out the very threads that make it possible', 'in God, truth, justice and mercy are not ingredients held in careful balance but one with His very being, and on the Cross that one love gave itself whole, holding nothing back. The world keeps trying to build communion while knocking out the very pillars that hold it up'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 302, 'At Babel, humanity sought unity on its own terms—"let us make a name for ourselves" (Genesis 11:4)—grasping at heaven without submitting to God. It ended in confusion and scattering.', 'At Babel, humanity sought unity on its own terms: "Then they said, \'Come, let us build ourselves a city, and a tower with its top in the heavens, and let us make a name for ourselves, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.\'" (Genesis 11:4). They grasped at heaven without submitting to God, and it ended in confusion and in the very scattering they feared.'), ('7 · Scripture whole or cf., in RSV wording', 302, '(Acts 2:1-11)', '(cf. Acts 2:1-11)'), ('3 · “Four pillars,” not threads (item 1)', 307, "lacks one of love's four threads, and so it cannot last.", "lacks one of love's four pillars, and so it cannot last."), ('5 · Let the Church explain: marriage, the Eucharist, unity', 314, 'The devil wants nothing more than denominations and arguments over truth.', 'The devil wants nothing more than a divided Christianity and endless arguments over truth.'), ('10 · Small fixes: plain words and accuracy', 333, 'Culture of Death that treats life as problem.', 'culture of death that treats life as a problem.'), ('4 · Banned words and overclaims a censor would notice (item 2)', 341, 'Not perfect. Not sinless. But **whole**.', 'Not flawless. Not sinless. But **whole**.'), ('9 · Structure: the Bridge names Chapter 11 (item 7)', 354, '## The Journey Continues', '## The Bridge to Prudence'), ('9 · Structure: the Bridge names Chapter 11 (item 7)', 356, 'into life-giving communion. **Truth** (Ch 6)', 'into life-giving communion. Love is **sacred** (Ch 3), a **gift of covenant** (Ch 4) that **binds** (Ch 5). **Truth** (Ch 6)'), ('9 · Structure: the Bridge names Chapter 11 (item 7)', 358, 'Each facet is essential. Remove any one, and love becomes something less than love. Together, they form', 'Together, the four pillars form'), ('9 · Structure: the Bridge names Chapter 11 (item 7)', 360, "Now we need to see how this love has been revealed, how it's made accessible, how it transforms us, and how it calls all Christians to unity.", 'Now we need to see how this love is lived, day by day, and what happens when it fails.'), ('9 · Structure: the Bridge names Chapter 11 (item 7)', 362, 'This is where Part III begins—applying this definition to key Christian questions and showing how it unlocks the deepest truths of our faith.', 'This is where Part III begins. In Chapter 11, we turn to prudence: the virtue that teaches us *how* to love, so that truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are lived as one act toward the real person in front of us.'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 388, '- **CCC 2331-2400** - The vocation to chastity, especially', '- **CCC 2331-2400** - The sixth commandment: chastity and the love of husband and wife, especially'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 392, '- **St. Augustine of Hippo (354-430)** - His *Confessions* Book XIII describes how we become what we eat in the Eucharist; his *City of God* describes the Church as a communion of love', '- **St. Augustine (354-430), Bishop of Hippo and Doctor of the Church** - In his *Confessions* (VII.10.16) he hears Christ say, "you shall be changed into Me"; preaching to the newly baptized (Sermon 272, quoted in CCC 1396), he teaches that our "Amen" at Communion says yes to the Body we are; his *City of God* (XIV.28) traces the two cities formed by two loves, love of God and love of self'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 393, '- **Pope St. John Paul II (1920-2005)** - His teaching', '- **St. John Paul II (1920-2005), Pope from 1978 to 2005** - His teaching'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 393, "shows how human sexuality participates in God's creative love", "shows how human sexuality participates in God's creative love; *Evangelium Vitae* (1995) names the culture of death and calls us to build a culture of life"), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 394, '- **Joseph Cardinal Ratzinger/Pope Benedict XVI (1927-2022)** -', '- **Pope Benedict XVI (Joseph Ratzinger, 1927-2022), Pope from 2005 to 2013** -'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 395, '- **Pope Paul VI (1897-1978)** - *Humanae Vitae* prophetically', '- **St. Paul VI (1897-1978), Pope from 1963 to 1978, canonized in 2018** - *Humanae Vitae* (1968) prophetically'), ('8 · Proper titles and corrected sources (items 3 and 4, todo 127)', 396, '- **St. Thomas Aquinas (1225-1274)** -', '- **St. Thomas Aquinas (c. 1225-1274), Dominican friar and Doctor of the Church** -')]
